# 8. P1 — Context Management

## 8.1 Context Manager

* [x] Context Budget — runtime/crates/agent-context: a window, a threshold to compact at, and a target to come down to (so one compaction buys several rounds instead of thrashing)
* [x] Token Budget — estimated at 4 chars/token, the same fallback the TS tokenizer uses; the budget's 15% headroom is far larger than the error
* [x] Context Priority — §8.2's four tiers, assigned by whoever created the item rather than inferred from its text
* [x] Context Lifecycle — items enter at a tier, are elided or compressed by budget pressure, and task state outlives all of it
* [x] Context Compaction — TypeScript (contextCompress.ts), and now in Rust (agent-context::compact), wired into the loop through ContextStrategy
* [x] Prefix Cache optimization — docs/prompt-cache-optimization.md
* [x] Tool Result lifecycle management — tool-output capping, test/tool-output-truncation.test.mjs

## 8.2 Context Tiers

### CRITICAL

* User Goal
* Task Goal
* Current Plan
* Hard Constraints

### HIGH

* Important Decisions
* Important Facts
* Current Progress

### NORMAL

* Conversation Context
* Normal Model Output

### EPHEMERAL

* Tool Output
* Temporary Search Results
* Temporary Debug Information

## 8.3 Compaction

When Context approaches its budget:

```text
Context
  ↓
Budget Check
  ↓
Compaction
  ↓
Preserve CRITICAL
  ↓
Preserve HIGH
  ↓
Compress NORMAL
  ↓
Remove EPHEMERAL
```

### Critical Requirement

Compaction must preserve:

* [x] User Goal — held in TaskMemory, outside the conversation, so compaction cannot reach it
* [x] Current Plan — same, and replaced rather than appended so two plans never disagree
* [x] Current Phase — a TaskMemory field, rendered into the wire on every request
* [x] Completed Tasks — a TaskMemory field
* [x] Pending Tasks — a TaskMemory field
* [x] Important Decisions — recorded in TaskMemory; the High tier also exists for a turn a caller wants kept whole
* [x] Important Constraints — recorded in TaskMemory. An agent that loses one of these does the thing it was told not to, confidently

The Agent must not lose its task state after compaction.

---

# 9. P1 — Agent Loop Stability

## 9.1 Stop Policy

* [x] Max Turns — stopPolicy.ts, and now agent-loop/src/stop.rs; unbounded by default on purpose, bounded for sub-agents
* [x] Max Tool Calls — same
* [x] Task Timeout — a wall-clock ceiling for the whole run; unbounded by default for the main agent (the user is watching), 15 min for a delegation (nobody is)
* [x] Runtime Timeout — read as a per-ROUND ceiling, bounded at 10 min even for the main agent: a round that has not finished is stuck rather than slow, and that is a different failure from a long task
* [x] User Cancellation — the existing per-conversation AbortController, ranked above every other stop reason

## 9.2 Doom Loop Detection

Detect:

* [x] Repeated Tool Calls — src/lib/agent/doomLoop.ts, ported to agent-loop/src/doom.rs
* [x] Repeated Calls with Identical Arguments
* [x] Repeated Resource Access
* [x] Repeated Searches
* [x] Equivalent Paths
* [x] Repeated Equivalent Operations

Recommended escalation:

```text
First occurrence
 ↓
Reminder

Repeated occurrence
 ↓
Warning

Continued repetition
 ↓
Stop
```

## 9.3 Error Recovery

* [x] Empty Model Response — an empty reply after real work nudges and goes round again
* [x] Invalid Tool Call — src/lib/ai/toolArgs.ts, ported to agent-dispatch/src/args.rs
* [x] Tool Failure
* [x] Model Failure
* [x] MCP Failure — a tool call never throws; ToolCallOutcome has no error variant
* [x] Runtime Failure — fail-open to the JS handler
* [x] Process Failure
* [x] IPC Failure — before the request is written it falls back; after it is written it reports a failure rather than re-running a side effect

---

# 10. P1 — Electron / Rust IPC

## 10.1 JSON-RPC

* [x] Request
* [x] Response
* [x] Error
* [x] Event — runtime.event carries every structured state change the runtime publishes
* [x] Streaming — agent.run pushes `agent.delta` notifications carrying INCREMENTS (sending the accumulation would be quadratic in the reply's length). Every delta is flushed before the run answers, so a client never sees the finished text before its tokens
* [x] Cancellation — call.cancel
* [x] Reconnect — handlers are registered on the module so they survive a restart
* [x] Runtime Status — runtime.status

## 10.2 Agent Events

Standardize Runtime Events:

```text
AgentStarted
AgentThinking
ToolStarted
ToolPermission
ToolRunning
ToolCompleted
ToolFailed
SubAgentStarted
SubAgentCompleted
McpConnected
McpDisconnected
PermissionDenied
AgentCompacted
AgentCompleted
AgentFailed
AgentCancelled
```

## 10.3 Runtime Crash Recovery

* [x] Electron detects Runtime status
* [x] Runtime crash detection
* [x] Runtime restart — 3 consecutive spawn failures stop the retry, so a broken sidecar is not a fork bomb
* [x] Task recovery — runtime/crates/agent-journal: an append-only journal, replayed at startup and reported in the handshake. Split by whether a task had BEGUN: queued work is resumable, started work is reported and never re-run
* [x] IPC reconnect
* [~] UI state restoration — the runtime reports what the last run left unfinished (InitializeResult.recovered, surfaced by rustRuntime.mjs); no UI consumes it yet

Target:

```text
Electron Crash
      ↓
Rust Runtime
      ↓
Continue / Recover Task
```

The Runtime should not depend on the Electron UI remaining alive.

