# 11. P2 — Audit / Observability

## 11.1 Execution IDs

Each task should have:

```text
Task ID
Agent ID
Sub-agent ID
Tool Execution ID
Process ID
MCP Request ID
```

## 11.2 Execution Timeline

Example:

```text
Task
 ├── Model Call
 ├── Tool Call
 │    ├── Permission
 │    ├── Sandbox
 │    └── Process
 ├── Sub-agent
 │    ├── Tool
 │    └── Tool
 └── Final Result
```

## 11.3 Audit Events

Record:

* [x] Agent Task — agent-audit is subscribed to the event bus and its percentiles are reported in runtime.status
* [~] Model Call — model calls happen in the runtime as of 6b; they are not yet audited
* [x] Tool Call — tool duration by name, derived from the event stream
* [x] Permission Decision — audited by PermissionRuntime at the one decision point
* [x] Sandbox Decision — a SandboxDecided event per command, carrying what was actually enforced. "Not requested" and "requested and unavailable" are recorded as different facts
* [x] Process Event — exit notifications plus a SandboxDecided event per command
* [x] MCP Event — an McpCalled event on every outcome, including "tried to call a server that is not connected", which is exactly what an audit trail exists to show
* [~] Sub-agent Event
* [~] Error
* [x] Cancellation — TaskCancelRequested and TaskCancelled, which is what makes propagation latency measurable rather than zero by construction
* [x] Runtime Event — every EventKind reaches the host as runtime.event

---

# 12. P2 — Security Hardening

Complete security validation for:

* [x] Path Traversal — agent-sandbox/tests/escape.rs
* [x] Symlink Escape — symlink laundering against a confined child
* [x] Sandbox Escape
* [x] Command Injection — run_command takes a shell line BY DESIGN, so the tests pin the narrower claims that matter: metacharacters execute as written, a command printing a protocol frame cannot inject one, and confinement is not escaped
* [x] Prompt Injection — justification_cannot_influence_a_decision: eight injection strings must all produce identical outcomes
* [x] Capability Escalation — in-crate
* [x] Sub-agent Privilege Escalation — derive_child tests
* [x] MCP Permission Bypass — tested against a really connected server: an unapproved one is denied and nothing is sent
* [x] Process Escape — grandchild delegation escape tests
* [~] Sensitive File Access — covered by the filesystem scope tests, not by a sensitive-path suite
* [x] Audit Integrity — the journal is a hash chain: editing, deleting or reordering a record is detected and located. TAMPER-EVIDENT, NOT TAMPER-PROOF — see §0.2 F8

All security tests must:

* [x] Pass locally
* [x] Run automatically in CI — cargo test in .github/workflows/ci.yml on all three platforms
* [x] Pass before every release — release.yml runs cargo test --release before packaging

---

# 13. P2 — Performance / Stability

## Runtime

* [~] Runtime Startup Benchmark — the IPC round-trip floor was measured once (0.48ms p50, stage1 §4); no standing benchmark
* [x] Runtime Memory Benchmark — `npm run bench:runtime memory`: resident set before and after a burst of concurrent work
* [x] Runtime CPU Benchmark — `bench:runtime cpu`, from /proc; reports null rather than guessing on platforms where it cannot be read
* [x] Runtime Shutdown Benchmark — and it found a real cost: a clean stop took 2008ms because `shutdown_timeout(2s)` waited out the blocking stdin read. Now 8.8ms

## Tools

* [x] File Tool Benchmark — scripts/ab-runtime-parity.mjs --bench
* [x] Search Benchmark — 2.37x on ext4
* [x] Command Benchmark — `bench:runtime command`, against Node's own child_process as the baseline it replaces
* [x] Concurrent Tool Benchmark — `bench:runtime tools`: the same calls serially and fanned out, so the concurrency is a number rather than a claim

## Agent

* [x] Long-running Task Test — agent-loop/tests/stress.rs: 200 rounds, with counters and transcript asserted consistent at the end
* [x] 100+ Tool Call Test — 120 calls across 12 fan-out rounds; every call is asserted to still have its result, in order
* [x] Large Context Test — 60 rounds against a 4000-token window; every request is asserted inside it, and the task state survives all of it
* [x] Compaction Test — test/context-manager.test.mjs
* [x] Multi-agent Test — the sub-agent suites
* [~] MCP Stress Test — fault injection, not load

## Stability

* [x] Memory Leak Test — stress.rs leak soak, plus agent-process's 40-run zombie soak
* [x] Zombie Process Test
* [~] Runtime Crash Test — the fail-open paths are tested; there is no crash-under-load recovery test
* [~] IPC Disconnect Test
* [x] MCP Disconnect Test — agent-mcp/tests/mcp.rs
* [x] Sub-agent Crash Test

## Platforms

* [~] Windows — CI builds the sidecar and runs the Rust tests plus A/B parity; no packaged-app pass
* [~] macOS Intel — same
* [~] macOS Apple Silicon — same
* [~] Linux — same

